# resources_heap_paging: A private heap of four pages over patterned guest bytes, two buffers placed in it (type 14, at 0 and at 0x8000) and a tracked private heap (flag bit 1) with one. Listing the heap pages it in, so a copy out of a heap buffer shows the guest bytes at its offset; a fill of a heap buffer stays on the host until the heap is listed with flags 6, or marked with 52 and synchronized with 53. The legacy heap's synchronize sets its host validity, not its guest validity, so a second 53 copies again. A heap buffer does not page (its prepare and synchronize are empty). 63 deletes an in-process heap buffer (it does not support discard) and discards the heap; 62 synchronizes and discards. In the service every write is in guest memory at once.
